# Image Assets — WebP First

When replacing or adding raster images under `public/`, **never commit PNG or JPG as the final asset**. Convert to WebP first, then reference the `.webp` path in components.

## Workflow

1. Save the source PNG/JPG temporarily (chat attachment, Downloads, or Figma export).
2. Convert with `sharp-cli` from the project root:

```bash
# Card / section images
npx --yes sharp-cli -i "path/to/source.png" -o "public/.../asset.webp" -f webp -q 90

# Full-bleed heroes
npx --yes sharp-cli -i "path/to/source.png" -o "public/.../hero.webp" -f webp -q 95
```

3. Update `<Image src="...">` (or data arrays) to point at the `.webp` file.
4. Delete the intermediate PNG/JPG from `public/` — do not leave both formats.
5. Set `quality` on `<Image>` when needed (`90` editorial, `95` heroes). Allowed values are in `next.config.ts` → `images.qualities`.

## Quality tiers

| Use case | sharp `-q` | `<Image quality>` |
|---|---|---|
| Cards, thumbnails, sections | 90 | 90 |
| Full-bleed heroes | 95 | 95 |
| Default / body | — | 80 (default) |

## Exceptions (keep original format)

- `opengraph-image.*`, `twitter-image.*`, `apple-icon.*` — Next.js metadata conventions
- `manifest.ts` icon entries
- Brand assets that require PNG transparency in non-WebP contexts (rare)

## Anti-patterns

- Referencing `.png` / `.jpg` in `public/` from components
- Using chat-attached images as source — Cursor downscales them (~50%); use Desktop/Downloads originals instead
- Pre-compressing to WebP then letting `/_next/image` re-compress at `q=80` without raising `quality`
- Leaving duplicate PNG + WebP pairs in `public/`
- Reusing old filenames when replacing images — use a new name to bust Next.js/browser cache
